/P 643 0 R
/T (Holding Period The holding period is the number of years that have passed between the date of acquisition and the exit date. To consider the decimals after the year count, we take the number of months in the holding period and divide it by 12 to get a decimal which gets added back to the year to get to the total holding period or each park.)
/E (Holding Period The holding period is the number of years that have passed between the date of acquisition and the exit date. To consider the decimals after the year count, we take the number of months in the holding period and divide it by 12 to get a decimal which gets added back to the year to get to the total holding period or each park.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1632 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 643 0 R
/T (Equity Multiple \(EM\) The equity multiple \(EM\) measures how much money an investor could make on their initial investment. An equity multiple less than 1.0X means you receive less cash than you invested. An equity multiple greater than 1.0X means you get more cash than initially invested. For example, an equity multiple of 2.25X implies for every $1 invested into the project, you can expect 2.25X in return.)
/E (Equity Multiple \(EM\) The equity multiple \(EM\) measures how much money an investor could make on their initial investment. An equity multiple less than 1.0X means you receive less cash than you invested. An equity multiple greater than 1.0X means you get more cash than initially invested. For example, an equity multiple of 2.25X implies for every $1 invested into the project, you can expect 2.25X in return.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1633 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 643 0 R
/T (Cash-on-Cash Return \(CoC\) Cash-on-cash return is the rate of return for a real estate transaction that calculates the cash income earned to the cash invested within the property. CoC return measures the annual return the investor made on the property in relation to the amount invested during the same time frame. For our purposes, this excludes sale proceeds on the property.)
/E (Cash-on-Cash Return \(CoC\) Cash-on-cash return is the rate of return for a real estate transaction that calculates the cash income earned to the cash invested within the property. CoC return measures the annual return the investor made on the property in relation to the amount invested during the same time frame. For our purposes, this excludes sale proceeds on the property.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1634 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 643 0 R
/T (Gross EM Gross equity multiple is calculated by summing the total sales proceeds of the park with the total amount of money distributed and then dividing despite total equity invested. Gross is on property level but takes into account property level fees, acquisition fees, management fees, property management fees, and can include deposition fees.)
/E (Gross EM Gross equity multiple is calculated by summing the total sales proceeds of the park with the total amount of money distributed and then dividing despite total equity invested. Gross is on property level but takes into account property level fees, acquisition fees, management fees, property management fees, and can include deposition fees.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1635 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 643 0 R
/T (Gross CoC Gross cash on cash is the percentage cash return investor receives on their initial cash investment. Gross CoC is calculated by dividing the money distributed over the investments life by the total equity invested, then dividing this again by the holding period. Gross is on property level  but takes in property level fees, acquisition fees, asset management fees, property management fees, and can include disposition fees.)
/E (Gross CoC Gross cash on cash is the percentage cash return investor receives on their initial cash investment. Gross CoC is calculated by dividing the money distributed over the investments life by the total equity invested, then dividing this again by the holding period. Gross is on property level  but takes in property level fees, acquisition fees, asset management fees, property management fees, and can include disposition fees.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1636 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 643 0 R
/T (Gross AAR Gross average annual return is solved by dividing the gross total return by the investment holding period. The resulting value is an annualized percentage and how much the rest return the park made during the investment duration. Gross is on property level but takes in property level fees, acquisition fees, asset management fees, property management fees, and can include disposition fees.)
/E (Gross AAR Gross average annual return is solved by dividing the gross total return by the investment holding period. The resulting value is an annualized percentage and how much the rest return the park made during the investment duration. Gross is on property level but takes in property level fees, acquisition fees, asset management fees, property management fees, and can include disposition fees.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1637 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 643 0 R
/T (Gross Total Return Gross total return is calculated by combining sales proceeds and the total money distributed, including property fees, less total equity raise. This number is divided by the equity raised to generate a percentage return over the life of the property. Gross is on property level but takes in property level fees, acquisition fees, asset management fees, property management fees, and can include disposition fees.)
/E (Gross Total Return Gross total return is calculated by combining sales proceeds and the total money distributed, including property fees, less total equity raise. This number is divided by the equity raised to generate a percentage return over the life of the property. Gross is on property level but takes in property level fees, acquisition fees, asset management fees, property management fees, and can include disposition fees.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [8]
>>
